      "title": "Hypothetical Local LLM Inference: Beyond Raw Tokens/Sec",
      "body": "I'm exploring hypothetical local LLM inference speeds and wanted to discuss what raw token generation figures might miss. In a controlled, hypothetical test on the same hardware, two runtimes produced 18 and 24 tokens per second respectively (F1, F2). While a clear difference, this benchmark is incomplete. Crucially, neither the time to first token (TTFT) nor the actual quality of the generated answers were assessed (F3). A faster TTFT can significantly impact perceived responsiveness, even with a slightly lower tokens/sec rate. Similarly, a model that generates tokens slower but provides more coherent or accurate output might be preferable. This limited hypothetical scenario doesn't establish a universal winner (F4), as other factors are paramount. What other metrics do you consider essential when evaluating local LLM performance beyond just raw throughput?",
