        "F4"
      ],
      "plan": "To initiate a discussion on the multifaceted nature of local LLM performance evaluation, going beyond simple token-per-second metrics. The post will highlight the limitations of the hypothetical benchmark and prompt the community to share their essential evaluation criteria."
    },
    {
      "title": "Hypothetical Local Inference: What Do 18 vs. 24 Tokens/Sec Really Mean?",
      "body": "Considering a hypothetical scenario for local LLM inference, I'm pondering the practical implications of varying token generation speeds. A simulated test on identical hardware yielded hypothetical speeds of 18 tokens/sec and 24 tokens/sec (F1, F2). While 24 tokens/sec is numerically higher, this snapshot lacks critical context. The hypothetical experiment did not capture the time it took to generate the first token, nor did it evaluate the semantic quality or relevance of the output (F3). A system with a slightly lower token rate but a much faster initial response might feel more interactive. Furthermore, this specific hypothetical test doesn't allow us to declare one as definitively superior for all use cases (F4). It's a reminder that benchmarks need careful interpretation. What aspects of local model performance, beyond raw throughput, are most important for your workflow?",
